-Two approaches remain credible:
+A retry with the same idempotency key and identical payload returns the original acceptance result; reuse of an accepted key with a different payload is rejected and leaves the original submission unchanged.
 
-- Whole-visit revisions: each edit names its starting revision. Concurrent edits produce competing complete revisions for a coordinator to resolve. This preserves the worker's submitted visit as a unit and needs relatively little merge logic, but even edits to unrelated fields create review work. Tablet-sync would retain base revision IDs and display pending competing revisions. Review-export would need a revision comparison workflow and exclude unresolved visits from its export set.
-- Field-aware three-way merge: each edit supplies its base state and changes. Disjoint field changes combine automatically; overlapping changes require review. This reduces review volume and gets more visits ready for export, but requires maintained rules for scalar fields, observer lists, and species entries. Automatically combined fields can also cross scientific relationships the rules do not understand. Tablet-sync would retain base snapshots and send structured changes. Review-export would need field-level provenance and a review view for overlapping changes.
+Current-state retrieval returns the current revision's fields and indicates whether unresolved review work exists for the visit. Submission-history retrieval returns each accepted revision with its author, receipt time, and base revision ID. Review-outcome operations read a visit's unresolved work and record a coordinator's resolution.
 
-The choice trades review workload and submission size against implementation burden and preservation of whole-visit intent. Neither approach may discard original submissions. Field-aware merging is not ruled out by a requirement that every combined record receive manual approval.
+## Unresolved Work and Resolution
+
+A visit is **resolved** when its current revision represents the accepted state. A visit is **unresolved** when one or more competing revisions await a coordinator. A visit's unresolved-work representation exposes its competing revision IDs so review-export can compare them and hold the visit out of the export set.
+
+A coordinator resolves an unresolved visit by designating one competing revision as the resolved current revision. Resolution makes that revision current, marks the visit resolved, and records the coordinator's identity and the contributing submission IDs. All original submissions remain stored after resolution.
 
 ## Downstream Contract Work
 
-After selection, define the submission envelope, current-state response, unresolved-work representation, and resolution operation here. Tablet-sync will size its local storage and retry state machine around that envelope. Review-export will size its coordinator interface and export eligibility rules around the unresolved-work representation. Both must link back to this node rather than invent their own concurrency semantics.
+The submission envelope, current-state response, unresolved-work representation, and resolution operation defined here are the contract tablet-sync and review-export build against. Tablet-sync retains base revision IDs and displays pending competing revisions. Review-export needs a revision comparison workflow and holds unresolved visits out of the export set. Both link back to this node rather than define their own concurrency semantics.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Concurrent-edit policy | Whole-visit revisions | Field-aware three-way merge | Preserves each worker's submission as one indivisible visit, so a scientific relationship the server cannot infer (such as a species identification and its cover estimate) is never split by an automatic merge. Trades a larger coordinator review queue and larger submissions for the absence of maintained merge rules. |
+| Concurrency detection | Compare each submission's base revision with the visit's current revision | Content- or field-based conflict detection | Base-lineage comparison is exact and needs no rules about which fields are related. It flags unrelated-field edits as conflicts too, which is the accepted cost of never merging. |
+| Resolution semantics | Coordinator designates one competing revision as current | Coordinator composes a new merged revision | Keeps every resolved value traceable to a worker's submitted visit and avoids writing coordinator-authored content through the submission path. Original submissions are retained regardless. |
+| Revision identity | Server-assigned revision ID; submitter-supplied base revision ID | Client-assigned revision IDs | The server is the single authority for identity, so two tablets cannot mint colliding revision IDs for the same visit. |
+
